(The greater part of the following treatise remains in the exact form) Tj T*
(in which it was read at Manchester; but the more familiar passages of) Tj T*
(it, which were trusted to extempore delivery, have been since written) Tj T*
(with greater explicitness and fullness than I could give them in) Tj T*
(speaking; and a considerable number of notes are added, to explain the) Tj T*
(points which could not be sufficiently considered in the time I had at) Tj T*
(my disposal in the lecture-room.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some apology may be thought due to the reader, for an endeavour to) Tj T*
(engage his attention on a subject of which no profound study seems) Tj T*
(compatible with the work in which I am usually employed. But profound) Tj T*
(study is not, in this case, necessary either to writer or reader,) Tj T*
(while accurate study, up to a certain point, is necessary for us all.) Tj T*
(Political economy means, in plain English, nothing more than) Tj T*
("citizens' economy"; and its first principles ought, therefore, to be) Tj T*
(understood by all who mean to take the responsibility of citizens, as) Tj T*
(those of household economy by all who take the responsibility of) Tj T*
(householders. Nor are its first principles in the least obscure: they) Tj T*
(are, many of them, disagreeable in their practical requirements, and) Tj T*
(people in general pretend that they cannot understand, because they) Tj T*
(are unwilling to obey them; or, rather, by habitual disobedience,) Tj T*
(destroy their capacity of understanding them. But there is not one of) Tj T*
(the really great principles of the science which is either obscure or) Tj T*
(disputable--which might not be taught to a youth as soon as he can be) Tj T*
(trusted with an annual allowance, or to a young lady as soon as she is) Tj T*
(of age to be taken into counsel by the housekeeper.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I might, with more appearance of justice, be blamed for thinking it) Tj T*
(necessary to enforce what everybody is supposed to know. But this) Tj T*
