(LECTURE I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the various characteristics of the age in which we live, as) Tj T*
(compared with other ages of this not yet _very_ experienced world, one) Tj T*
(of the most notable appears to me to be the just and wholesome) Tj T*
(contempt in which we hold poverty. I repeat, the _just_ and) Tj T*
(_wholesome_ contempt; though I see that some of my hearers look) Tj T*
(surprised at the expression. I assure them, I use it in sincerity; and) Tj T*
(I should not have ventured to ask you to listen to me this evening,) Tj T*
(unless I had entertained a profound respect for wealth--true wealth,) Tj T*
(that is to say; for, of course, we ought to respect neither wealth nor) Tj T*
(anything else that is false of its kind: and the distinction between) Tj T*
(real and false wealth is one of the points on which I shall have a few) Tj T*
(words presently to say to you. But true wealth I hold, as I said, in) Tj T*
(great honour; and sympathize, for the most part, with that) Tj T*
(extraordinary feeling of the present age which publicly pays this) Tj T*
(honour to riches. I cannot, however, help noticing how extraordinary) Tj T*
(it is, and how this epoch of ours differs from all bygone epochs in) Tj T*
(having no philosophical nor religious worshippers of the ragged) Tj T*
(godship of poverty. In the classical ages, not only there were people) Tj T*
