(Economy no more means saving money than it means spending money. It) Tj T*
(means, the administration of a house; its stewardship; spending or) Tj T*
(saving, that is, whether money or time, or anything else, to the best) Tj T*
(possible advantage. In the simplest and clearest definition of it,) Tj T*
(economy, whether public or private, means the wise management of) Tj T*
(labour; and it means this mainly in three senses: namely, first,) Tj T*
(_applying_ your labour rationally; secondly, _preserving_ its produce) Tj T*
(carefully; lastly, _distributing_ its produce seasonably.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I say first, applying your labour rationally; that is, so as to obtain) Tj T*
(the most precious things you can, and the most lasting things, by it:) Tj T*
(not growing oats in land where you can grow wheat, nor putting fine) Tj T*
(embroidery on a stuff that will not wear. Secondly, preserving its) Tj T*
(produce carefully; that is to say, laying up your wheat wisely in) Tj T*
(storehouses for the time of famine, and keeping your embroidery) Tj T*
(watchfully from the moth: and lastly, distributing its produce) Tj T*
(seasonably; that is to say, being able to carry your corn at once to) Tj T*
(the place where the people are hungry, and your embroideries to the) Tj T*
(places where they are gay, so fulfilling in all ways the Wise Man's) Tj T*
(description, whether of the queenly housewife or queenly nation. "She) Tj T*
(riseth while it is yet night, and giveth meat to her household, and a) Tj T*
(portion to her maidens. She maketh herself coverings of tapestry, her) Tj T*
(clothing is silk and purple. Strength and honour are in her clothing,) Tj T*
(and she shall rejoice in time to come.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now, you will observe that in this description of the perfect) Tj T*
(economist, or mistress of a household, there is a studied expression) Tj T*
(of the balanced division of her care between the two great objects of) Tj T*
(utility and splendour; in her right hand, food and flax, for life and) Tj T*
(clothing; in her left hand, the purple and the needlework, for honour) Tj T*
(and for beauty. All perfect housewifery or national economy is known) Tj T*
(by these two divisions; wherever either is wanting, the economy is) Tj T*
(imperfect. If the motive of pomp prevails, and the care of the) Tj T*
(national economist is directed only to the accumulation of gold, and) Tj T*
(of pictures, and of silk and marble, you know at once that the time) Tj T*
(must soon come when all these treasures shall be scattered and blasted) Tj T*
(in national ruin. If, on the contrary, the element of utility) Tj T*
(prevails, and the nation disdains to occupy itself in any wise with) Tj T*
(the arts of beauty or delight, not only a certain quantity of its) Tj T*
(energy calculated for exercise in those arts alone must be entirely) Tj T*
(wasted, which is bad economy, but also the passions connected with the) Tj T*
(utilities of property become morbidly strong, and a mean lust of) Tj T*
(accumulation merely for the sake of accumulation, or even of labour) Tj T*
(merely for the sake of labour, will banish at last the serenity and) Tj T*
(the morality of life, as completely, and perhaps more ignobly, than) Tj T*
(even the lavishness of pride and the lightness of pleasure. And) Tj T*
(similarly, and much more visibly, in private and household economy,) Tj T*
(you may judge always of its perfectness by its fair balance between) Tj T*
(the use and the pleasure of its possessions. You will see the wise) Tj T*
