(selfish and base one: good art, as well as other good things, ought to) Tj T*
(be made as cheap as possible, and put as far as we can within the) Tj T*
(reach of everybody.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Pardon me, I am not prepared to admit that. I rather side with the) Tj T*
(selfish objectors, and believe that art ought not to be made cheap,) Tj T*
(beyond a certain point; for the amount of pleasure that you can) Tj T*
(receive from any great work, depends wholly on the quantity of) Tj T*
(attention and energy of mind you can bring to bear upon it. Now, that) Tj T*
(attention and energy depend much more on the freshness of the thing) Tj T*
(than you would at all suppose; unless you very carefully studied the) Tj T*
(movements of your own minds. If you see things of the same kind and of) Tj T*
(equal value very frequently, your reverence for them is infallibly) Tj T*
(diminished, your powers of attention get gradually wearied, and your) Tj T*
(interest and enthusiasm worn out; and you cannot in that state bring) Tj T*
(to any given work the energy necessary to enjoy it. If, indeed, the) Tj T*
(question were only between enjoying a great many pictures each a) Tj T*
(little, or one picture very much, the sum of enjoyment being in each) Tj T*
(case the same, you might rationally desire to possess rather the) Tj T*
