() Tj T*
(Under this head, we shall have to examine the laws of currency and) Tj T*
(exchange; of which I will note here the first principles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Money has been inaccurately spoken of as merely a means of) Tj T*
(circulation. It is, on the contrary, an expression of right. It is not) Tj T*
(wealth, being the sign[69] of the relative quantities of it, to which,) Tj T*
(at a given time, persons or societies are entitled.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(   [69] Always, and necessarily, an imperfect sign; but capable) Tj T*
(        of approximate accuracy if rightly ordered.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If all the money in the world, notes and gold, were destroyed in an) Tj T*
(instant, it would leave the world neither richer nor poorer than it) Tj T*
(was. But it would leave the individual inhabitants of it in different) Tj T*
(relations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Money is, therefore, correspondent in its nature to the title-deed of) Tj T*
(an estate. Though the deed be burned, the estate still exists, but the) Tj T*
(right to it has become disputable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The worth of money remains unchanged, as long as the proportion of the) Tj T*
(quantity of existing money to the quantity of existing wealth, or) Tj T*
(available labour which it professes to represent, remains unchanged.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the wealth increases, but not the money, the worth of the money) Tj T*
(increases; if the money increases, but not the wealth, the worth of) Tj T*
(the money diminishes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
