(Money, therefore, cannot be arbitrarily multiplied, any more than) Tj T*
(title-deeds can. So long as the existing wealth or available labour is) Tj T*
(not fully represented by the currency, the currency may be increased) Tj T*
(without diminution of the assigned worth of its pieces. But when the) Tj T*
(existing wealth, or available labour, is once fully represented, every) Tj T*
(piece of money thrown into circulation diminishes the worth of every) Tj T*
(other existing piece, in the proportion it bears to the number of) Tj T*
(them, provided the new piece be received with equal credit; if not,) Tj T*
(the depreciation of worth takes place exclusively in the new piece,) Tj T*
(according to the inferiority of its credit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When, however, new money, composed of some substance of supposed) Tj T*
(intrinsic value \(as of gold\), is brought into the market, or when new) Tj T*
(notes are issued which are supposed to be deserving of credit, the) Tj T*
(desire to obtain money will, under certain circumstances, stimulate) Tj T*
(industry; an additional quantity of wealth is immediately produced,) Tj T*
(and if this be in proportion to the new claims advanced, the value of) Tj T*
(the existing currency is undepreciated. If the stimulus given be so) Tj T*
(great as to produce more goods than are proportioned to the additional) Tj T*
(coinage, the worth of the existing currency will be raised.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Arbitrary control and issues of currency affect the production of) Tj T*
(wealth, by acting on the hopes and fears of men; and are, under) Tj T*
(certain circumstances, wise. But the issue of additional currency to) Tj T*
(meet the exigencies of immediate expense, is merely one of the) Tj T*
(disguised forms of borrowing or taxing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is, however, in the present low state of economical knowledge,) Tj T*
(often possible for Governments to venture on an issue of currency,) Tj T*
(when they could not venture on an additional loan or tax, because the) Tj T*
(real operation of such issue is not understood by the people, and the) Tj T*
(pressure of it is irregularly distributed, and with an unperceived) Tj T*
(gradation. Finally, the use of substances of intrinsic value as the) Tj T*
(materials of a currency, is a barbarism;--a remnant of the conditions) Tj T*
(of barter, which alone can render commerce possible among savage) Tj T*
(nations. It is, however, still necessary, partly as a mechanical check) Tj T*
(on arbitrary issues; partly as a means of exchanges with foreign) Tj T*
(nations. In proportion to the extension of civilization, and increase) Tj T*
(of trustworthiness in Governments, it will cease. So long as it) Tj T*
(exists, the phenomena of the cost and price of the articles used for) Tj T*
(currency, are mingled with those of currency itself, in an almost) Tj T*
(inextricable manner; and the worth of money in the market is affected) Tj T*
(by multitudinous accidental circumstances, which have been traced,) Tj T*
(with more or less success, by writers on commercial operations; but) Tj T*
