(The last paper having consisted of little more than definition of) Tj T*
(terms, I purpose, in this, to expand and illustrate the given) Tj T*
(definitions, so as to avoid confusion in their use when we enter into) Tj T*
(the detail of our subject.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The view which has been taken of the nature of wealth, namely, that it) Tj T*
(consists in an intrinsic value developed by a vital power, is directly) Tj T*
(opposed to two nearly universal conceptions of wealth. In the) Tj T*
(assertion that value is primarily intrinsic, it opposes the idea that) Tj T*
(anything which is an object of desire to numbers, and is limited in) Tj T*
(quantity, may be called, or virtually become, wealth. And in the) Tj T*
(assertion that value is secondarily dependent upon power in the) Tj T*
(possessor, it opposes the idea that wealth consists of things) Tj T*
(exchangeable at rated prices. Before going farther, we will make these) Tj T*
(two positions clearer.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First. All wealth is intrinsic, and is not constituted by the judgment) Tj T*
(of men. This is easily seen in the case of things affecting the body;) Tj T*
(we know that no force of fantasy will make stones nourishing, or) Tj T*
(poison innocent; but it is less apparent in things affecting the mind.) Tj T*
(We are easily--perhaps willingly--misled by the appearance of) Tj T*
(beneficial results obtained by industries addressed wholly to the) Tj T*
(gratification of fanciful desire; and apt to suppose that whatever is) Tj T*
(widely coveted, dearly bought, and pleasurable in possession, must be) Tj T*
(included in our definition of wealth. It is the more difficult to quit) Tj T*
(ourselves of this error because many things which are true wealth in) Tj T*
(moderate use, yet become false wealth in immoderate; and many things) Tj T*
(are mixed of good and evil,--as, mostly, books and works of art,--out) Tj T*
(of which one person will get the good, and another the evil; so that) Tj T*
(it seems as if there were no fixed good or evil in the things) Tj T*
(themselves, but only in the view taken, and use made of them. But that) Tj T*
(is not so. The evil and good are fixed in essence and in proportion.) Tj T*
(They are separable by instinct and judgment, but not interchangeable;) Tj T*
(and in things in which evil depends upon excess, the point of excess,) Tj T*
(though indefinable, is fixed; and the power of the thing is on the) Tj T*
(hither side for good, and on the farther side for evil. And in all) Tj T*
(cases this power is inherent, not dependent on opinion or choice. Our) Tj T*
(thoughts of things neither make, nor mar their eternal force;) Tj T*
(nor--which is the most serious point for future consideration--can) Tj T*
(they prevent the effect of it upon ourselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Therefore, the object of special analysis of wealth into which we have) Tj T*
(presently to enter will be not so much to enumerate what is) Tj T*
(serviceable, as to distinguish what is destructive; and to show that) Tj T*
(it is inevitably destructive; that to receive pleasure from an evil) Tj T*
(thing is not to escape from, or alter the evil of it, but to be) Tj T*
(altered by it; that is, to suffer from it to the utmost, having our) Tj T*
(own nature, in that degree, made evil also. And it will be shown) Tj T*
(farther that, through whatever length of time or subtleties of) Tj T*
(connexion the harm is accomplished \(being also less or more according) Tj T*
(to the fineness and worth of the humanity on which it is wrought\),) Tj T*
