(In this view there are three errors. The first and chief is the) Tj T*
(overlooking the fact that all exchangeableness of commodity, or) Tj T*
(effective demand for it, depends on the sum of capacity for its use) Tj T*
(existing, here or elsewhere. The book we cannot read, or picture we) Tj T*
(take no delight in, may indeed be called part of our wealth, in so far) Tj T*
(as we have power of exchanging either for something we like better.) Tj T*
(But our power of effecting such exchange, and yet more, of effecting) Tj T*
(it to advantage, depends absolutely on the number of accessible) Tj T*
(persons who can understand the book, or enjoy the painting, and who) Tj T*
(will dispute the possession of them. Thus the actual worth of either,) Tj T*
(even to us, depends no less on their essential goodness than on the) Tj T*
(capacity consisting somewhere for the perception of it; and it is vain) Tj T*
(in any completed system of production to think of obtaining one) Tj T*
(without the other. So that, though the great political economist knows) Tj T*
(that co-existence of capacity for use with temporary possession cannot) Tj T*
(be always secured, the final fact, on which he bases all action and) Tj T*
(administration, is that, in the whole nation, or group of nations, he) Tj T*
(has to deal with, for every grain of intrinsic value produced he must) Tj T*
(with exactest chemistry produce its twin grain of governing capacity,) Tj T*
(or in the degrees of his failure he has no wealth. Nature's challenge) Tj T*
(to us is in earnest, as the Assyrian's mock, "I will give you two) Tj T*
(thousand horses, if thou be able on thy part to set riders upon them.") Tj T*
(Bavieca's paces are brave, if the Cid backs him; but woe to us, if we) Tj T*
(take the dust of capacity, wearing the armour of it, for capacity) Tj T*
(itself, for so all procession, however goodly in the show of it, is to) Tj T*
(the tomb.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The second error in this popular view of wealth is that, in estimating) Tj T*
(property which we cannot use as wealth, because it is exchangeable, we) Tj T*
(in reality confuse wealth with money. The land we have no skill to) Tj T*
(cultivate, the book which is sealed to us, or dress which is) Tj T*
(superfluous, may indeed be exchangeable, but as such are nothing more) Tj T*
(than a cumbrous form of bank-note, of doubtful and slow) Tj T*
(convertibility. As long as we retain possession of them, we merely) Tj T*
(keep our bank-notes in the shape of gravel or clay, of book leaves, or) Tj T*
(of embroidered tissue. Circumstances may perhaps render such forms the) Tj T*
(safest, or a certain complacency may attach to the exhibition of) Tj T*
(them;--into both these advantages we shall inquire afterwards; I wish) Tj T*
(the reader only to observe here, that exchangeable property which we) Tj T*
(cannot use is, to us personally, merely one of the forms of money, not) Tj T*
(of wealth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The third error in the popular view is the confusion of guardianship) Tj T*
(with possession; the real state of men of property being, too commonly) Tj T*
(that of curators, not possessors of wealth. For a man's power of Use,) Tj T*
(Administration, Ostentation, Destruction, or Bequest; and possession) Tj T*
(is in use only, which for each man is sternly limited; so that such) Tj T*
