(Quit of these three sources of misconception, the reader will have) Tj T*
(little farther difficulty in apprehending the real nature of Effectual) Tj T*
(value. He may, however, at first not without surprise, perceive the) Tj T*
(consequences involved in the acceptance of our definition. For if the) Tj T*
(actual existence of wealth be dependent on the power of its possessor,) Tj T*
(it follows that the sum of wealth held by the nation, instead of being) Tj T*
(constant or calculable, varies hourly, nay, momentarily, with the) Tj T*
(number and character of its holders; and that in changing hands, it) Tj T*
(changes in quantity. And farther, since the worth of the currency is) Tj T*
(proportioned to the sum of material wealth which it represents, if the) Tj T*
(sum of the wealth changes, the worth of the currency changes. And thus) Tj T*
(both the sum of the property, and power of the currency, of the State,) Tj T*
(vary momentarily, as the character and number of the holders. And not) Tj T*
(only so, but a different rate and manner of variation is caused by the) Tj T*
(character of the holders of different kinds of wealth. The transitions) Tj T*
(of value caused by the character of the holders of land differ in mode) Tj T*
(from those caused by character in holders of works of art; and these) Tj T*
(again from those caused by character in holders of machinery or other) Tj T*
(working capital. But we cannot examine these special phenomena of any) Tj T*
(kind of wealth until we have a clear idea of the way in which true) Tj T*
(currency expresses them; and of the resulting modes in which the cost) Tj T*
(and price of any article are related to its value. To obtain this we) Tj T*
(must approach the subject in its first elements.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let us suppose a national store of wealth, real or imaginary \(that is) Tj T*
(to say, composed of material things either useful, or believed to be) Tj T*
(so\), presided over by a Government,[74] and that every workman, having) Tj T*
(produced any article involving labour in its production, and for which) Tj T*
(he has no immediate use, brings it to add to this store, receiving,) Tj T*
(from the Government, in exchange an order either for the return of the) Tj T*
(thing itself, or of its equivalent in other things,[75] such as he may) Tj T*
(choose out of the store at any time when he needs them. Now, supposing) Tj T*
(that the labourer speedily uses this general order, or, in common) Tj T*
(language, "spends the money," he has neither changed the circumstances) Tj T*
(of the nation nor his own, except in so far as he may have produced) Tj T*
(useful and consumed useless articles, or vice versa. But if he does) Tj T*
(not use, or uses in part only, the order he receives, and lays aside) Tj T*
(some portion of it; and thus every day bringing his contribution to) Tj T*
(the national store, lays by some percentage of the order received in) Tj T*
(exchange for it, he increases the national wealth daily by as much as) Tj T*
(he does not use of the received order, and to the same amount) Tj T*
(accumulates a monetary claim on the Government. It is of course always) Tj T*
(in his power, as it is his legal right, to bring forward this) Tj T*
(accumulation of claim, and at once to consume, to destroy, or) Tj T*
(distribute, the sum of his wealth. Supposing he never does so, but) Tj T*
(dies, leaving his claim to others, he has enriched the State during) Tj T*
(his life by the quantity of wealth over which that claim extends, or) Tj T*
(has, in other words, rendered so much additional life possible in the) Tj T*
(State, of which additional life he bequeaths the immediate possibility) Tj T*
(to those whom he invests with his claim, he would distribute this) Tj T*
(possibility of life among the nation at large.) Tj T*
