(Now, if for this conception of a central Government, we substitute) Tj T*
(that of another body of persons occupied in industrial pursuits, of) Tj T*
(whom each adds in his private capacity to the common store: so that) Tj T*
(the store itself, instead of remaining a public property of) Tj T*
(ascertainable quantity, for the guardianship of which a body of public) Tj T*
(men are responsible, becomes disseminated private property, each man) Tj T*
(giving in exchange for any article received from another, a general) Tj T*
(order for its equivalent in whatever other article the claimant may) Tj T*
(desire \(such general order being payable by any member of the society) Tj T*
(in whose possession the demanded article may be found\), we at once) Tj T*
(obtain an approximation to the actual condition of a civilized) Tj T*
(mercantile community from which approximation we might easily proceed) Tj T*
(into still completer analysis. I purpose, however, to arrive at every) Tj T*
(result by the gradual expansion of the simpler conception; but I wish) Tj T*
(the reader to observe, in the meantime, that both the social) Tj T*
(conditions thus supposed \(and I will by anticipation say also all) Tj T*
(possible social conditions\) agree in two great points; namely, in the) Tj T*
(primal importance of the supposed national store or stock, and in its) Tj T*
(destructibility or improvability by the holders of it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I. Observe that in both conditions, that of central) Tj T*
(Government-holding, and diffused private-holding, the quantity of) Tj T*
(stock is of the same national moment. In the one case, indeed, its) Tj T*
(amount may be known by examination of the persons to whom it is) Tj T*
(confided; in the other it cannot be known but by exposing the private) Tj T*
(affairs of every individual. But, known or unknown, its significance) Tj T*
(is the same under each condition. The riches of the nation consist in) Tj T*
(the abundance, and their wealth depends on the nature of this store.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(II. In the second place, both conditions \(and all other possible ones\)) Tj T*
(agree in the destructibility or improvability of the store by its) Tj T*
(holders. Whether in private hands, or under Government charge, the) Tj T*
(national store may be daily consumed, or daily enlarged, by its) Tj T*
(possessors; and while the currency remains apparently unaltered, the) Tj T*
(property it represents may diminish or increase.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first question, then, which we have to put under our simple) Tj T*
(conception of central Government, namely, "What store has it?" is one) Tj T*
(of equal importance, whatever may be the constitution of the State;) Tj T*
(while the second question--namely, "Who are the holders of the) Tj T*
(store?"--involves the discussion of the constitution of the State) Tj T*
(itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first inquiry resolves itself into three heads:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  1. What is the nature of the store?) Tj T*
(  2. What is its quantity in relation to the population?) Tj T*
(  3. What is its quantity in relation to the currency?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The second inquiry, into two:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  1. Who are the Holders of the store, and in what proportions?) Tj T*
