(openly demanded, because it is the reward of the merchant's knowledge,) Tj T*
(and foresight of probable necessity; but the greater part of such gain) Tj T*
(is unjust; and unjust in this most fatal way, that it depends first on) Tj T*
(keeping the exchangers ignorant of the exchange value of the articles,) Tj T*
(and secondly, on taking advantage of the buyer's need and the seller's) Tj T*
(poverty. It is, therefore, one of the essential, and quite the most) Tj T*
(fatal, forms of usury; for usury means merely taking an exorbitant sum) Tj T*
(for the use of anything, and it is no matter whether the exorbitance) Tj T*
(is on loan or exchange, in rent or in price--the essence of the usury) Tj T*
(being that it is obtained by advantage of opportunity or necessity,) Tj T*
(and not as due reward for labour. All the great thinkers, therefore,) Tj T*
(have held it to be unnatural and impious, in so far as it feeds on) Tj T*
(the distress of others, or their folly.[97] Nevertheless attempts to) Tj T*
