BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(     of her fig-tree; but as I should have had to go on to the) Tj T*
(     lovely myth of Leucothea's veil, and did not care to spoil) Tj T*
(     this by a hurried account of it, I left it for future) Tj T*
(     examination; and three days after the paper was published,) Tj T*
(     observed that the reviewers, with their usual useful) Tj T*
(     ingenuity, were endeavouring to throw the whole subject back) Tj T*
(     into confusion by dwelling on the single \(as they imagined\)) Tj T*
(     oversight. I omitted also a note on the sense of the word) Tj T*
(     [Greek: lygron], with respect to the pharmacy of Circe, and) Tj T*
(     herb-fields of Helen \(compare its use in Odyssey, xvii. 473,) Tj T*
(     etc.\), which would further have illustrated the nature of) Tj T*
(     the Circean power. But, not to be led too far into the) Tj T*
(     subtleness of these myths, respecting them all I have but) Tj T*
(     this to say: Even in very simple parables, it is not always) Tj T*
(     easy to attach indisputable meaning to every part of them. I) Tj T*
(     recollect some years ago, throwing an assembly of learned) Tj T*
(     persons who had met to delight themselves with) Tj T*
(     interpretations of the parable of the prodigal son) Tj T*
(     \(interpretations which had up to that moment gone very) Tj T*
(     smoothly\) into high indignation, by inadvertently asking who) Tj T*
(     the prodigal son was, and what was to be learned by his) Tj T*
(     example. The leading divine of the company \(still one of our) Tj T*
(     great popular preachers\) at last explained to me that the) Tj T*
(     unprodigal son was a lay figure, put in for dramatic effect,) Tj T*
(     to make the story prettier, and that no note was to be taken) Tj T*
(     of him. Without, however, admitting that Homer put in the) Tj T*
(     last escape of Ulysses merely to make his story prettier,) Tj T*
(     this is nevertheless true of all Greek myths, that they have) Tj T*
(     many opposite lights and shades: they are as changeful as) Tj T*
(     opal and, like opal, usually have one colour by reflected,) Tj T*
(     and another by transmitted, light. But they are true jewels) Tj T*
(     for all that, and full of noble enchantment for those who) Tj T*
(     can use them; for those who cannot, I am content to repeat) Tj T*
(     the words I wrote four years ago, in the appendix to the) Tj T*
(     "Two Paths"--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     "The entire purpose of a great thinker may be difficult to) Tj T*
(     fathom, and we may be over and over again more or less) Tj T*
(     mistaken in guessing at his meaning; but the real, profound,) Tj T*
(     nay, quite bottomless and unredeemable mistake, is the) Tj T*
(     fool's thought, that he had no meaning.") Tj T*
